6. AI FINDINGS, ROOT-CAUSE ANALYSIS, AND HUMAN CONTROL

6.1. LLMs and AI-assisted analytical components are probabilistic and may produce inaccurate, false, incomplete, misleading, outdated, inconsistent, or fabricated content, even when presented with apparently credible evidence.

6.2. An incident report, diagnostic conclusion, proposed root cause, affected-component assessment, severity rating, or recommendation generated by the Software is a hypothesis or decision-support output, not an authoritative statement of fact unless independently verified.

6.3. The Software DOES NOT GUARANTEE that an incident will be detected; that a root cause or contributing factor will be identified; that an identified cause is correct or complete; that a suggested action will be safe or effective; or that outages, losses, data corruption, or security incidents will be prevented. The absence of a finding is not proof of service health or security.

6.4. Final responsibility for assessing evidence, validating hypotheses, deciding whether and how to act, and authorizing production changes remains with the Licensee’s qualified human personnel. The final decision must be made by a human, not by the Software.

6.5. The Software is not intended to autonomously restart services, change configurations, delete or modify business data, deploy fixes, modify network policies, or execute other remediation actions. No AI output constitutes approval or authorization for such an action.

6.6. Before implementing any recommendation that may affect service availability, data, access controls, system integrity, or security, the Licensee must use suitable human review, independent evidence, change management, backups, and rollback procedures.

6.7. Installing the Software does not make the Licensor the Licensee’s infrastructure operator, incident commander, security monitor, managed service provider, or incident-response team.

6.8. Nothing here relieves the Licensor of responsibility for its own unlawful conduct or defects to the extent that such responsibility cannot lawfully be excluded.

6.9. Logs, alerts, traces, external documents and tool outputs may contain incorrect, malicious, misleading or instruction-like content, including attempts to manipulate an LLM (“prompt injection”). Such content may affect the completeness or reliability of AI analysis or recommendations. The Licensee must treat third-party evidence and generated outputs as untrusted until independently validated, and must not rely on the Software to prevent every such manipulation.

6.10. UTU Engine is an AI-enabled investigation platform. AI-generated findings, interpretations, hypotheses and recommendations are decision-support material and, where technically practicable, are identified as AI-assisted within the Software. The Licensee must not describe such outputs as independently verified human findings unless a qualified person has actually verified them. Qualified human personnel retain responsibility for evaluating evidence and approving operational decisions under Sections 6.2–6.6. Nothing in this Agreement transfers or disclaims mandatory transparency, risk-management or other legal duties imposed on either party by applicable artificial-intelligence legislation.

7. DIAGNOSTIC ACCESS, READ-ONLY DEFAULT, AND ACTIVE PROBES

7.1. The Licensee selects and configures connected systems and warrants that it has lawful authorization to access, collect, and analyze the relevant information. Permissions should be restricted to the minimum required for each connector and diagnostic tool.

7.2. The ordinary automated investigation workflow is intended to be READ-ONLY with respect to the Licensee’s application and business data. The Software must not be regarded as an automatic remediation system. Read-only access, however, does not mean that diagnostic queries or network requests are passive or free of operational effect.

7.3. Some optional functions may constitute active diagnostics or “Deep Probes”, including network connection tests, predefined SSH commands, diagnostic workloads, and creation or deletion of temporary Kubernetes Jobs or Pods. These functions may have effects beyond reading stored evidence and must be disabled by default unless deliberately enabled or invoked by an appropriately authorized Licensee.

7.4. Before enabling an active diagnostic function, the Licensee must examine its specific behavior, permissions, scope, expected side effects, network paths, resource impact, and any applicable documented warnings. Authorization to retrieve evidence is not blanket authorization for all possible active probes.

7.5. Active probes and repeated connection attempts may be interpreted by security controls or source systems as suspicious or abusive traffic. They may cause rate limiting, connection resets, service denial, temporary or permanent IP or account blocking, alerting, access revocation, or similar defensive responses. The Licensee accepts the operational risk of enabling and executing these functions, to the extent permitted by law.

7.6. The Licensee is responsible for applying least-privilege roles, network restrictions, execution limits, controlled testing, independent monitoring, backups, and change-management procedures appropriate to its environment.

7.7. A tool may fail, return partial data, omit a relevant source, or yield misleading diagnostic evidence. The Licensee must not assume that an unavailable, unconfigured, disabled, or failing connector participated in an investigation.

7.8. The Licensee must review the tool-specific risk documentation and warnings for each enabled connector or diagnostic tool. The documentation portal is identified as https://docs.utu-engine.com/. The detailed descriptions supplement, but do not override, this Agreement; material contractual terms require appropriate acceptance under Section 22.

7.9. The Licensee must independently verify a tool’s actual required privileges and behavior in its chosen configuration. A description such as “read-only” does not guarantee that every backend operation is side-effect-free, non-blocking, non-locking, non-invasive or harmless to capacity or security controls.

8. RESOURCE CONSUMPTION AND DEGRADATION OF CONNECTED BACKENDS

8.1. Diagnostic requests generated or initiated by UTU Engine can impose additional computational, storage, memory, I/O, network, connection, locking, and other operational load on connected backends, telemetry platforms, databases, hosts, clusters, and applications, even where the requested operation is nominally read-only.

8.2. The volume, intensity, and cumulative effects of that load have NOT BEEN comprehensively measured or benchmarked across all supported backends, configurations, workloads, and operating conditions. The Licensor does not represent that diagnostic activity has a zero, negligible, predictable, or universally safe resource cost.

8.3. Actual impact depends on multiple factors, including the type and number of requests; query scope, complexity, concurrency and frequency; data volume and retention; the number of investigations; network conditions; the configuration and efficiency of the connected services; other workloads; and, critically, the spare capacity and resources available on each affected backend at the relevant time.

8.4. Where a backend is resource-constrained, overloaded, degraded, or otherwise operating near its limits, even a relatively small diagnostic workload may contribute to increased latency, queueing, throttling, timeouts, elevated memory or CPU usage, additional disk or network pressure, service degradation, or temporary unavailability. In some circumstances, diagnostic activity may worsen an existing incident.

8.5. Before connecting production systems, the Licensee must evaluate capacity and risk, test relevant queries and probe functions in a controlled environment where practicable, apply suitable rate and concurrency restrictions and access controls, monitor resource usage, and suspend or limit diagnostic activity if adverse effects are observed.

8.6. By enabling a connector, initiating an investigation, or authorizing diagnostic requests, the Licensee acknowledges and knowingly accepts the foreseeable risk that such activity may burden or degrade its own or authorized third-party backends. This allocation of operational responsibility applies only to the extent permitted by law and does not excuse the Licensor’s own legally actionable conduct or non-excludable duties.

8.7. The Software is not guaranteed to detect or halt every harmful query pattern, resource spike, lock contention condition, or backend overload. Licensees must maintain independent safeguards and operational authority to stop diagnostics.

8.8. The Licensee acknowledges that investigation fan-out, retries, repeated diagnostic steps, alert-triggered investigations, connected-tool concurrency and automated queries may compound load, particularly during an ongoing outage. No universal safe request volume, rate, parallelism, or resource threshold is promised by this Agreement.

9. LICENSEE DATA, MASKING, AND CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION

9.1. A self-hosted deployment may process or store investigation data and evidence within infrastructure controlled by the Licensee, depending on the actual configuration. The Licensee retains all rights it lawfully holds in that data.

9.2. Logs, traces, metrics, diagnostic output, database responses, topology, configuration, prompts, and reports may contain personal data, credentials, access tokens, confidential business information, internal network details, or other sensitive material.

9.3. The Software may provide default and configurable or custom masking, filtering, redaction, or sanitization rules. These controls are best-effort and CANNOT GUARANTEE that every sensitive field, secret, identifier, or personal-data item will be detected or removed. Different formats, nested content, free-form text, errors, tool changes, and unknown data structures may bypass them.

9.4. The PRIMARY responsibility for data minimization and filtering is with the Licensee, including at the original source system, before the data becomes available to UTU Engine. The Licensee must determine which datasets may be retrieved and transmitted and restrict source-level permissions, fields, retention, and access accordingly.

9.5. Before retrieving production data or sending investigation context to an external AI or messaging provider, the Licensee must evaluate the data classes involved, apply source-level minimization, assess masking and filtering using representative inputs where practicable, and review generated requests and outputs where the Software makes this possible. Production configurations must not rely solely on a belief that masking removes all sensitive information.

9.6. The Licensee acknowledges the residual risk that unmasked personal data, secrets, or confidential content may be included in an AI request, retained in a provider’s system under its terms, or disclosed through a misconfiguration. The Licensee accepts the risks arising from its chosen configuration and authorized disclosure, to the extent permitted by law; this does not excuse unlawful processing or wrongful disclosures attributable to the Licensor.

9.7. The Licensee must establish a lawful basis for handling personal data, comply with applicable privacy and cross-border transfer requirements, administer access and retention, protect secrets, and investigate incidents affecting systems under its control.

9.8. The Licensor does not gain general ownership of, or an automatic right to inspect or reuse, Licensee data merely because the Software processed it. Applicable privacy notices and data-processing arrangements govern any information actually received by the Licensor.

9.9. Acceptance of this EULA by an administrator or organization is NOT A CONSENT on behalf of any data subject whose personal data may appear in diagnostic information. The Licensee must independently establish any required lawful basis, notices, consents, contractual authorizations and cross-border transfer safeguards before the data are transmitted.

9.10. The Licensee must not provide source-system rights or data to the Software that it lacks authority to process or disclose to its selected AI and other third-party providers. Where such authorization is not available, the Licensee must use a compliant configuration, limit the data, select an appropriate local provider or refrain from enabling the affected feature.

10. EXTERNAL AI AND THIRD-PARTY INTEGRATIONS

10.1. When the Licensee configures and uses an external LLM or AI API endpoint, the Software DIRECTLY TRANSMITS requests and relevant investigation context from the Licensee’s environment to that third-party endpoint. Depending on the investigation and configuration, transmitted content may include incident summaries and descriptions, alert labels, affected service and application identifiers, topology, infrastructure metadata, the Software’s diagnostic requests, and selected or condensed log, trace, metric, database, host, network and other tool results. The transmitted information MAY INCLUDE SENSITIVE DATA and may not be anonymized.

10.2. The Licensee selects or configures the AI provider and is responsible for evaluating its conditions of service, processing purposes, retention and deletion terms, any model-training or service-improvement use, security and access practices, subprocessors, processing locations, contractual safeguards and cross-border transfer obligations before enabling the connection.

10.3. Depending on the external provider’s terms and configuration, transmitted prompts and results may be logged, retained, reviewed for abuse prevention, processed by subcontractors, or used for service improvement or model training. The Licensor makes no representation that a provider will not retain or use such content. The Licensee must obtain and apply any provider-specific no-training, retention or confidentiality commitments it requires.

10.4. Any third-party API usage, model tokens, cloud resources, traffic, data storage, messaging, subscriptions and related charges are the Licensee’s responsibility. Free UTU Engine licensing does not make independent third-party services free. Third-party performance, responses, limits, pricing, availability, terms and policies may change or fail without notice to the Licensor.

10.5. The Licensor is not a party to the Licensee’s independent provider contracts, and does not control the provider’s data processing, security incidents, availability or output quality. This clause does not excuse unauthorized disclosure or legally actionable conduct attributable to the Licensor under applicable law.

10.6. External AI processing may occur automatically in response to an investigation or configured external trigger as well as after a manual initiation. The Licensee must ensure that authorized persons understand when a configured investigation can cause provider requests and must apply suitable access, trigger, routing and data-minimization controls.

10.7. The Licensee acknowledges that hosting UTU Engine itself inside its own infrastructure does not, by itself, keep all investigation information inside that infrastructure. Data may leave that environment through expressly configured LLM, notification, telemetry or other third-party integrations. Use of a genuinely local, appropriately isolated model may avoid one such external AI transfer, but other configured outbound integrations still require review.

10.8. Where notifications or reports are delivered through external email, chat, messaging or webhook providers selected by the Licensee, incident metadata, alert descriptions, application or service identifiers, summaries and investigation findings may be shared with those providers and with recipients configured by the Licensee. The Licensee is responsible for recipient authorization, channel confidentiality, retention, onward distribution and applicable legal requirements.

10.9. An EULA acknowledgement alone does not constitute a data-processing agreement, a standard contractual clause, a data-subject consent, or a legal basis for otherwise prohibited disclosure. The actual data-protection roles of the Licensor, Licensee and service providers must be assessed according to the facts and applicable law, not merely the terms “self-hosted” or “third-party provider”.

10.10. The Licensee should obtain sufficient technical and contractual information from each selected external provider before transmitting protected or regulated data. Where contractual safeguards or local-law permissions are absent, it must not route such data to that provider.

